{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"vigilant-erasable-pony","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# Bidirectional Markdown ⇄ Jira rich-text sync (Cloud ADF + DC wiki)\n\n## Context / Problem\nThe Jira reconciler syncs rebar ticket descriptions and comments as **plain text**, so Markdown structure authors write (headings, checklists, code, lists) shows up as literal source in Jira, and rich text authored in Jira reads as noise in rebar. We want each client to receive a native rich representation — **ADF** for Jira Cloud, **wiki markup** for Jira Data Center — without a sync echo loop that would ratchet Jira-specific formatting back into the store or erode content over repeated passes. Validated over the real historical corpus (1,771 tickets / 5,568 bodies) by six de-risking experiments recorded in session log `5eb0-52a3-45f0-43e5`.\n\n## Approach\nTwo asymmetric codecs behind one state-based echo-safety layer, because the two Jira targets are fundamentally different formats:\n- **Cloud = truly bidirectional, lossless.** ADF is structured JSON; `marklas` round-trips Markdown↔ADF at 99.64% fixed-point over the corpus — the structured-typed-node pattern, inherently echo-safe under normalize-both-sides.\n- **DC = one-way MD→wiki render, bidirectional SYNC.** Jira wiki is a lossy text markup and no library round-trips rebar's punctuation-dense prose reliably (pandoc's jira reader misparses `->`, ASCII tables, and regex, and provably erodes them one char per pass). So DC renders one way over the units that are structurally safe, passes dangerous units through verbatim, and prevents loops with **state-based echo suppression** using the reconciler's existing per-binding baseline (ADR 0026, `binding_store.py:102`) — NOT codec fixed-point. Genuine human Jira edits are still detected against baseline and pulled back best-effort.\n\nEcho-safety is state-based for both clients; concurrency is last-write-wins over a three-way state (baseline/local/remote) so neither side's changes are ever silently destroyed. State additions obey epic `0303`'s churn discipline (change-gated, fixed-size, never a per-pass timestamp).\n\n## Dependencies / sequencing\nThis epic must not be claimed until its three in-flight prerequisites close: `0303` (tickets-store growth / committed-state format), `e369` (Jira-family DC seam), `3e73` (Cloud↔DC test parity). These are recorded as `depends_on` links.\n\n## Child responsibilities (decomposition)\n- **Cloud ADF codec** — vendor/pin `marklas`; Markdown↔ADF for descriptions and comments; fix the combined strong+strikethrough mark-order oscillator; accept the ~18 transient whitespace canonicalizations via normalize-both-sides. Delivers AC1.\n- **DC one-way segmenting renderer** — split body into blocks/list-items, render structurally-safe units to wiki via pinned pandoc, substitute prose arrows on the Markdown BEFORE pandoc, verbatim-wrap ASCII tables in `{noformat}`, pass dangerous units (tables/arrows/HTML/regex) through unchanged, reassemble. Delivers AC2.\n- **DC robustness/perf hardening** — bound pandoc's per-field subprocess cost, add a hard timeout with plain-text fallback on pathological input, and satisfy pandoc GPL/SBOM compliance. Delivers AC2 under production constraints.\n- **State echo suppression + LWW conflict safety** — reuse the ADR-0026 baseline (observed-after-push canonical form) plus a change-gated inline `rich_sha`; three-way LWW that always flags concurrent conflict; origin-gated outbound so Jira-native constructs survive. Delivers AC3, AC4, AC5.\n- **Comment sync (append-only)** — persistent Jira-comment-ID map (replacing body-equality identity); rebar→Jira creation and inbound new-comment mirroring only (no edit/delete sync); descriptions cut over before comments. Delivers the comment portion of AC1/AC2.\n- **Live DC harness + cross-repo contract** — extend the existing Docker Jira DC harness to assert rendered-HTML fidelity (not just wiki source), and add a contract test pinning `normalize_rich_text` back-compat for the sibling `agentic-dev-platform` importer. Delivers AC6.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Cloud descriptions and comments sync as ADF and round-trip Markdown↔ADF with no perpetual re-sync over the historical corpus; verified by a corpus idempotence test that asserts convergence (incl. the two known combined-mark bodies).\n- [ ] DC descriptions and comments render to wiki for structurally-safe content while remaining echo-safe with zero content erosion over ≥5 repeated passes, including the known ASCII-table ratchet body; verified by a corpus echo-safety test asserting no monotonic length decay.\n- [ ] A reconcile pass in which no body changed writes 0 new committed-state entries and no per-pass timestamp attributable to this feature; verified by the git-delta cost harness reproducing the ≤0.07 KB/version stable profile.\n- [ ] A concurrent edit on both sides is always detected and flagged rather than silently overwritten; verified by the concurrent-conflict regression test over the corpus (the proven 799/799-detected invariant).\n- [ ] A Jira-authored native construct (panel/mention/date) survives a subsequent rebar-originated reconcile pass; verified by an origin-gating test.\n- [ ] The end-to-end behavior runs against a live Jira DC instance asserting rendered HTML (via `?expand=renderedFields`), not just wiki source; verified by the extended Docker DC harness CI job.","id":"708d-0767-1a51-4928","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["rich-text","jira","reconciler"],"ticket_type":"epic","title":"Bidirectional Markdown ⇄ Jira rich-text sync (Cloud ADF + DC wiki)"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785800266820717001,"uuid":"b45df4cb-669e-4abd-8638-9c5fd99a5785"}